      {
        "id": 134117,
        "name": "Nataraja",
        "username": "Nataraja",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/n/7feea3/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2015-02-27T18:09:26.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Donner\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/donner/48/1903_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Donner:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It doesn't seem like a mess to me.The first ones have to hit X targets. &nbsp;That means they can't hit the same target twice, but they can hit more targets if they haven't reached X yet. &nbsp;And they are not particular about which targets they're hitting.The second ones Say hit a target for X. &nbsp;Then hit a target for Y. &nbsp;Those are separate attacks. &nbsp;They can hit the same target twice.The third one targets a set of targets (X targets) with a HP criteria (all highest, all lowest, etc.). &nbsp;It HAS to snapshot at this point to figure out everyone that fits its criteria. &nbsp;Trying to re-calculate the criteria every time a new target comes into play becomes MUCH more complex than taking the targets as they were and dealing out the damage to them.This is, in my opinion, the cleaner way to do things. &nbsp;Everything else gets messier when you start adding the other interactions possible in the game.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>That's why I say it's a mess. The third one behaves differently because it has to or the game breaks. Or at least gets under a lot of stress. It would be more elegant to normalize the game so third case isn't a case at all and doesn't have to stand out as behaving differently and we have as much of everything behaving consistently as we can.</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:09:54.475Z",
